## First challenge: the Socratic already contains the Pragmatic

<Sophisticated Socratic Questioning already includes the Pragmatic>: A leading work of analytic metaphysics asks a Socratic question and counts practical payoff among its criteria, so the two kinds of questioning are not found apart.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 372", note: "Refutation by counterexample: one worked case is enough to show that the two kinds of questioning are not in fact pursued separately."}

<Sophisticated Socratic Questioning already includes the Pragmatic>

(1) [Paul and Hall ask a Socratic question]: Paul and Hall's book deals with the question 'What is causation?', a Socratic question if there ever was one. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 372", source: "\"deals with the question 'What is causation?' a Socratic question if there ever was one\""}
(2) [They weigh more than intuitive fit]: Paul and Hall discuss a number of considerations relevant to a good analysis besides fit with intuitive case judgments. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 372", source: "\"Paul and Hall discuss a number of considerations relevant to a good analysis besides fit with intuitive case judgments\""}
(3) [Those considerations include practical payoff]: Among the considerations Paul and Hall weigh are fit with scientific practice, fit into a broader metaphysical theory, illuminating some aspect of scientific inquiry, and the way science helps us practically. #reported
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 372", source: "\"including (i) fit with scientific practice, (ii) fit into a broader metaphysical theory, (iii) illuminating some aspect of scientific inquiry\"", note: "The fourth item, `the way science helps us practically`, follows a footnote marker and is quoted separately in the source text; it is the item that carries the point."}
-----
(4) [Socratic Questioning already includes Pragmatic Questioning]: A sophisticated approach to Socratic Questioning does not neglect considerations of how concepts help serve practical needs, but rather already includes them.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 372", source: "\"a sophisticated approach to Socratic Questioning does not neglect considerations of how concepts help serve practical needs, but rather already includes them\""}

[Socratic Questioning already includes Pragmatic Questioning]
    <- [It includes them differently] #conceded

[It includes them differently]: Sophisticated Socratic Questioning includes practical considerations in a way different from the way suggested by Pragmatic Genealogists. #conceded
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 372", source: "\"it includes them in a way different from the way suggested by Pragmatic Genealogists\"", note: "The author's own `however`, and it cuts against him here: if the inclusion is different in kind, the counterexample shows less than it seems to. He answers it later by saying the difference is one of modesty (n. 15), which is the conclusion he wants anyway."}

## Second challenge: Pragmatic Questions presuppose Socratic answers

<Pragmatic Questions presuppose Socratic answers>: An illuminating pragmatic account of the separation of powers turns on ideals whose nature only Socratic inquiry can settle.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 373", note: "Argument from a single worked example to a general claim about `many` Pragmatic Questions. The author's own scope word is kept."}

<Pragmatic Questions presuppose Socratic answers>

(1) [Möllers on the separation of powers]: According to Möllers, the division of powers into legislative, executive and judicial branches functions to help negotiate tensions between modern ideals of individual and collective self-determination. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 373", source: "\"this division functions to help negotiate tensions between modern ideals of individual and collective self-determination\""}
(2) [Self-determination ideals are not self-interpreting]: Ideals like individual and collective self-determination are not self-interpreting.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 373", source: "\"ideals like individual and collective self-determination are not self-interpreting\""}
(3) [Understanding their nature is Socratic]: Understanding the nature of ideals like individual and collective self-determination is a paradigmatic Socratic exercise.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 373", source: "\"understanding their nature seems like a paradigmatic Socratic exercise\""}
-----
(4) [This Pragmatic Question presupposes Socratic answers]: The answer to the Pragmatic Question about the separation of powers presupposes answers to other Socratic Questions.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 373", source: "\"it seems the answer to this Pragmatic Question presupposes answers to other Socratic Questions\""}

[Answering Pragmatic Questions often requires Socratic answers]: Posing and answering many Pragmatic Questions often requires independently answering philosophically substantive Socratic Questions.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 373", source: "\"posing and answering many Pragmatic Questions often requires independently answering philosophically substantive Socratic Questions\"", note: "The general claim is stated first and the separation of powers is offered to illustrate it, so it is drawn as a thesis the worked case supports rather than as a step inferred from the single case. The author's `many` and `often` are his."}
    <+ [This Pragmatic Question presupposes Socratic answers]

[Serving is not merely causal-functional]: The way the different branches of government serve the needs for collective and individual self-determination is not merely causal-functional; there are constitutive and symbolic connections between these institutions and these ideals that seem relevant.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 373", source: "\"there are other constitutive and symbolic connections between these institutions and these ideals that seem relevant\""}

[Pragmatic Genealogy cannot illuminate constitutive relations]: Constitutive and symbolic connections are not things that Pragmatic Genealogies are in a good position to illuminate.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 373", source: "\"nor are they things that Pragmatic Genealogies are in a good position to illuminate\"", note: "Raised here in passing — `a point to which I will return` — and cashed out on p. 383. It is the one claim this map uses in two sections, which is why the paper's last objection is not a new one."}
    <+ [Serving is not merely causal-functional]

[Socratic theorizing discerns constitutive relations]: Constitutive and symbolic connections seem the kind of relations we are better able to discern in the process of broader Socratic theorizing.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 373", source: "\"they seem the kind of relations we are better able to discern in the process of broader Socratic theorizing\""}
    <+ [Pragmatic Genealogy cannot illuminate constitutive relations]
    +> [Answering Pragmatic Questions often requires Socratic answers]

## Third challenge: the two questions operate at different levels

<The genealogy of justice gives only a job description>: Hume's genealogy tells us what job justice does, and Socratic questions about justice ask how that job should be done.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 374"}

<The genealogy of justice gives only a job description>

(1) [Hume on justice]: According to Hume, the artificial virtue of justice functions to prevent conflicts over resources in conditions of scarcity and limited benevolence. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 374", source: "\"functions to prevent conflicts over resources in conditions of scarcity and limited benevolence\""}
(2) [Only the job description]: All that Hume's genealogy of justice tells us is the broad job description of justice.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 374", source: "\"all this genealogy tells us is the broad job description of justice\""}
(3) [Not how the job is performed]: Hume's genealogy does not tell us how the job of justice should be performed.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 374", source: "\"how that job should be performed\"", note: "The author's sentence here reads `it does not us tell us how that job should be performed` — a transposition in the printed text, so only the clean clause is quoted."}
-----
(4) [Concept, not conception]: We have a genealogy of the concept of justice, but what Socratic questions about justice often aim to illuminate is the correct conception of justice.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 374", source: "\"we have a genealogy of the concept of justice, but what Socratic questions about justice often aim to illuminate is the correct conception of justice\""}

<Genealogies of norm concepts find only the effects of agreement>: For concepts that pick out commonly accepted norms, what a pragmatic genealogy identifies is the benefit of having agreed rules at all, which is not what the content question asks about.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 374", note: "The generalisation of the Hume case: `This is not a local problem with Hume's particular genealogy of justice.`"}

