## What the models cannot do

<Pragmatic Genealogies assess concepts atomistically>: Because state-of-nature models look at a concept in isolation, they cannot see the practical good a concept does through its place in a theory.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 378", note: "The turn the paper makes at `let us leave these arguments aside`: from what the models are not needed for, to what they are not able to do. This is the argument that survives the concession above."}

<Pragmatic Genealogies assess concepts atomistically>

(1) [Genealogies assess atomistically]: Pragmatic Genealogies suggest an atomistic assessment of our conceptual practices.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 378", source: "\"they suggest an atomistic assessment of our conceptual practices\""}
(2) [Concepts examined in isolation]: Pragmatic Genealogies examine the effects of possessing certain concepts in isolation rather than looking at them in their role within broader theories.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 378", source: "\"they examine the effects of possessing certain concepts in isolation rather than looking at them in their role within broader theories\""}
(3) [Concepts can help indirectly]: A concept may help us practically in indirect ways, by clarifying aspects of scientific inquiry, or by being an essential element in a broader theory that, as a whole theory, helps us.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 378", source: "\"there are other, more indirect ways in which the concept of causation might help us practically: for example, by helping us clarify aspects of scientific inquiry... by being an essential element in a broader philosophy of science or metaphysical theory that, as a whole theory, helps us\"", note: "An elision within one sentence: the omitted span is the parenthetical example about drawing causal conclusions from statistical data."}
(4) [State-of-nature stories miss indirect roles]: State-of-nature stories do not help us understand such indirect practical roles for our concepts.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 378", source: "\"State-of-nature stories do not help us understand such indirect practical roles for our concepts\""}
-----
(5) [Fixation leaves out practical aid]: Fixation on state-of-nature stories ends up leaving out important ways in which conceptual practices may provide us with practical aid.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 378", source: "\"fixation on them ends up leaving out important ways in which conceptual practices may provide us with practical aid\""}

<A genealogical model could be contrived>: A state-of-nature model could after all be built for a concept whose value runs through a theory, by imagining a community that has the theory and then acquires the concept. #contested
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "n. 25", note: "The author raises the objection against himself in a footnote and answers it there. Left as a sketch: the footnote states the possibility and its answer, and giving the possibility premises would be the reconstruction's work."}

[One could imagine such a community]: One could imagine a community that had all the aspects of the relevant scientific, social or metaphysical theory but lacked the relevant concept, and then note the improved practical situation of the individuals after the concept was introduced. #contested
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "n. 25", source: "\"One could imagine a community that had all the aspects of the relevant scientific, social or metaphysical theory but lacked the relevant concept\""}

[A genealogical model could explain indirect functions]: One could construct a genealogical model that would explain the function of such concepts via their role in clarifying practically helpful theories. #contested
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "n. 25", source: "\"This is not to say that one could not construct a genealogical model that would 'explain' the function of such concepts via their role in clarifying practically helpful theories.\"", note: "The source states this as a double negative — what the author declines to say — so the claim text puts it positively and the quotation keeps the original form."}
    <+ [One could imagine such a community]

[State-of-nature stories miss indirect roles]
    <- <A genealogical model could be contrived>

<The apparatus does no work in a contrived model>: Even in such a model the state-of-nature machinery is idle, because everything is settled by a fit between concept and theory that is in place before the story begins.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "n. 25", note: "AN UNDERCUT. It concedes that the model can be built and denies that building it shows the apparatus does anything."}

