## Whether Williams on intrinsic value rescues the axiology

<Williams on intrinsic value rescues the axiology>: On Williams' account, what a Pragmatic Genealogy establishes is part of what makes something intrinsically valuable, so the method's focus on basic needs is no bar to a wide range of values. #reported
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 380-381", note: "Queloz's second reply, which the author says needs a bit of unpacking and then unpacks over a page."}

<Williams on intrinsic value rescues the axiology>

(1) [The Sense-Making Condition]: Individuals can coherently treat X as an intrinsic good, which is to say that they can make sense of X as an intrinsic value from the inside. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 380", source: "\"Individuals can coherently treat X as an intrinsic good, which is to say that they can make sense of X as an intrinsic value from the inside.\""}
(2) [The Practical Necessity Condition]: It is necessary, or nearly necessary, for basic human purposes and needs that human beings should treat X as an intrinsic good. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 380", source: "\"It is necessary (or nearly necessary) for basic human purposes and needs that human beings should treat X as an intrinsic good\""}
(3) [The two conditions are sufficient]: Williams, as reconstructed by Queloz, thinks these conditions are sufficient for something to have intrinsic value. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 380", source: "\"Williams (as reconstructed by Queloz) thinks the following conditions are sufficient for something to have intrinsic value\""}
(4) [Genealogy vindicates practical necessity]: A Pragmatic Genealogy can help vindicate the Practical Necessity Condition on intrinsic value. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"a Pragmatic Genealogy can help vindicate the Practical Necessity Condition on intrinsic value\""}
-----
(5) [Genealogy can vindicate intrinsic values]: Pragmatic Genealogy is not only compatible with a range of intrinsic values but can even help vindicate them. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"pragmatic genealogy is not only compatible with a range of intrinsic values but can even help vindicate them\""}

<The praying counterexample>: A practice can be coherently valued as an intrinsic good and be socially indispensable without being intrinsically valuable.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 381", note: "Refutation by counterexample against a sufficiency claim, and an ATTACK rather than an undercut: it denies a premise of Queloz's reply outright."}

<The praying counterexample>

(1) [A coherent practice of prayer]: Imagine a community that believes praying to Jesus Christ has intrinsic value, and this belief is embedded in a coherent set of mutually supporting attitudes and intentions.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"Imagine a community that believes that praying to Jesus Christ has intrinsic value, and this belief is embedded in a coherent set of mutually supporting attitudes and intentions.\"", note: "A supposition, entertained to construct the counterexample. Not asserted by the author."}
(2) [The practice is socially indispensable]: Imagine that this belief promotes charity, social harmony and existential consolation, and that absent the practice we would have selfishness, conflict and death-of-God wailing.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"it promotes charity, social harmony, existential consolation\"", note: "Also part of the supposition. It supplies the Practical Necessity Condition, as the first premise supplies the Sense-Making Condition."}
(3) [Prayer lacks intrinsic value]: Praying to Jesus Christ does not seem to have intrinsic value.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"Does praying to Jesus Christ, therefore, have intrinsic value? It does not seem so.\""}
-----
(4) [The conditions are not sufficient]: Williams' conditions are not plausible sufficient conditions for something having intrinsic value.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"Williams' conditions are not plausible sufficient conditions for something having intrinsic value\""}

[Prayer lacks intrinsic value]
    <+ [It depends on whether there is a Jesus Christ]

[It depends on whether there is a Jesus Christ]: Whether praying to Jesus Christ has intrinsic value depends on whether there is a Jesus Christ.
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"does not this depend on whether there is a Jesus Christ?\"", note: "Put as a rhetorical question, and read as its assertion because it is offered as a further reason for the verdict just given."}

[The two conditions are sufficient]
    <- <The praying counterexample>

<Axiological alchemy>: The two conditions have no independent plausibility and no reason to be conjoined, and the account needs a second, different theory of intrinsic value elsewhere.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 381"}

<Axiological alchemy>

(1) [Alchemy]: There seems to be a kind of axiological alchemy in concocting intrinsic value from intrinsic valuing plus practical necessity.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"More generally, there seems to be a kind of axiological alchemy in concocting intrinsic value from intrinsic valuing plus practical necessity.\"", note: "The quotation carries the author's `More generally`: this is the generalisation of the praying counterexample, not a fresh charge."}
(2) [Reverse-engineered in the bad sense]: Williams' account of intrinsic value seems reverse-engineered in the bad sense, designed to defuse an objection in an ad hoc manner.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"designed to defuse an objection in an ad hoc manner\""}
(3) [Two conceptions of intrinsic value are needed]: An entirely different view of intrinsic value is required when it comes to the intrinsic importance of basic needs.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"an entirely different view of intrinsic value is required when it comes to the intrinsic importance of basic needs\""}
-----
(4) [Williams' view is idiosyncratic and faces challenges]: Williams' view of intrinsic value is idiosyncratic and faces challenges.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"Williams' view of intrinsic value is idiosyncratic and faces challenges\""}

<Resting the credentials on Williams is risky>: Whatever the fate of Williams' account, a method's humanistic credentials should not hang on a theory of intrinsic value this idiosyncratic.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 381", note: "AN UNDERCUT, and the second of the author's two stated problems with Queloz's response. It does not require that Williams' view be false: it says that even a defensible version could not carry the dialectical weight Queloz puts on it. The author's `There is of course more to say here` is why the conclusion says risky rather than untenable."}

<Resting the credentials on Williams is risky>

(1) [Williams' view is idiosyncratic and faces challenges]
(2) [Odd to rest credentials on it]: It seems odd to let Pragmatic Genealogy's humanistic credentials rest on Williams' view of intrinsic value.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"it seems odd to let Pragmatic Genealogy's humanistic credentials rest on it\""}
-----
(3) [Resting the credentials on Williams is risky]: Resting Pragmatic Genealogy's humanistic credentials on Williams' view of intrinsic value seems risky.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 381", source: "\"resting Pragmatic Genealogy's humanistic credentials on this view seems risky\""}

