## Whether explanation without justification reconciles the two

<Explanation without justification reconciles the two>: An account of a practice's function can be an explanation rather than a justification and still do normative work that complements the humanist's own reasons. #reported
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 382", note: "Queloz's later paper on blame, which the author brings in himself as a possible route beyond the book."}

<Explanation without justification reconciles the two>

(1) [Blame's function]: Blame's function is to coordinate moral sensibilities by alerting and sanctioning those who are insufficiently responsive to the community's conception of moral reasons. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"blame's function is to coordinate moral sensibilities by alerting and sanctioning those who are insufficiently responsive to the community's conception of moral reasons\""}
(2) [Function explains, not justifies]: The account of blame's function serves not as a justification of the practice of blame but as an explanation of it, the practice being justified by the moral reasons that render individuals blameworthy. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"this account of blame's function serves not as a justification of the practice of blame but simply as a... explanation of the practice, the practice being justified by the moral reasons that render individuals blameworthy\"", note: "The elision skips the parenthetical `functional-causal`, which the printed text sets with a double hyphen the quotation checker cannot fold."}
(3) [An additional reason to cultivate blame]: The account gives us an additional reason to cultivate the practice of blame, since it solves a coordination problem. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"it gives us an additional reason to cultivate the practice of blame\""}
(4) [It strengthens confidence]: The account can strengthen our confidence in the practice of blame by indicating that it is not a hold-over from an enchanted world. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"it can 'strengthen our confidence' in the practice of blame by indicating that it is not a hold-over from an enchanted world\""}
(5) [It indicates pointlessness]: The account can indicate when participating in the practice might be pointless or counterproductive. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"it can indicate when participating in the practice might be pointless or counterproductive\""}
-----
(6) [Instrumental considerations complement humanism]: Instrumental considerations might complement rather than conflict with the humanist's focus on the internal standards and values of the practice. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"These are all ways that instrumental considerations might compliment rather than conflict with the humanist's focus on the internal standards and values of the practice.\"", note: "The printed text reads `compliment`; the quotation keeps it and the claim text reads `complement`."}

<Instrumental considerations create reengineering pressure>: An explanation that can raise our confidence in a practice can equally lower it for the parts of the practice that do not pay their way.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 382", note: "The author's hedge — `While I lack space to extensively argue the case, I am sceptical` — governs this whole line and is why its conclusion is about pressure rather than about refutation."}

<Instrumental considerations create reengineering pressure>

(1) [Normative work requires justificatory force]: It is hard for a causal explanation to serve these various normative functions without the explanatory considerations also in some sense being justificatory, that is, yielding reasons to engage in the practice or modify it in some way.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"it is hard for a causal explanation to serve these various normative functions without the explanatory considerations also in some sense being 'justificatory', that is, yielding reasons to engage in the practice or modify it in some way\""}
(2) [Not a concern for the first consideration]: This is not a big concern when it comes to Queloz's first consideration, since the additional instrumental reasons point in the same direction as the other reasons. #conceded
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"This is not a big concern when it comes to Queloz's first consideration, since the additional instrumental reasons point in the same direction as the other reasons.\"", note: "One of Queloz's three considerations is granted outright. Leaving the concession out would make the objection cover three points where the author presses it on two."}
(3) [Aspects of a practice may be hold-overs]: Even if instrumental considerations indicate that the practice as a whole is not a hold-over, they might suggest that many aspects of the practice are.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"even if instrumental considerations strengthen our confidence by indicating that the practice as a whole is not a hold-over, they might suggest that many aspects of the practice are\""}
(4) [Nature is not the perfect engineer]: After all, nature is not the perfect engineer.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"After all, nature is not the perfect engineer.\""}
(5) [Costs should decrease confidence]: If instrumental benefits are supposed to increase our confidence in a practice, comparative instrumental costs should correspondingly decrease our confidence in the aspects of the practice that are not instrumentally valuable.
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"should not comparative instrumental costs correspondingly decrease our confidence in the aspects of the practice that are not instrumentally valuable?\"", note: "Put as a rhetorical question. The sentence that follows draws the consequence, which fixes the answer as yes."}
-----
(6) [Pressure to reengineer]: Taking instrumental considerations seriously creates pressure to reengineer our practices to make them more instrumentally valuable.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"Taking instrumental considerations seriously creates pressure to reengineer our practices to make them more instrumentally valuable.\""}

<Either the pressure is real or the genealogy adds nothing>: If the instrumental considerations are weighty enough to matter they conflict with the humanist's reasons, and if they are not they add nothing to the humanist's resources.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 382", note: "A dilemma. The paper states the second horn explicitly and the first is what the previous argument establishes; the disjunction that makes them a dilemma is imputed below."}

<Either the pressure is real or the genealogy adds nothing>

(1) [Pressure to reengineer]
(2) [Either the pressure is real or the benefits are unimportant]: Either the pressure to reengineer our practices is real, or the instrumental benefits are not important enough to generate it.
    {fidelity: "imputation", warrant: "enthymeme", pinpoint: "p. 382", note: "Never stated. The paragraph moves straight from `Perhaps this pressure is very weak` to what follows if it is, and the argument is a dilemma only if the two horns exhaust the options."}
(3) [Weak pressure means unimportant benefits]: If the pressure is very weak, that is because the instrumental benefits are not very important, at least in comparison to other considerations.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"Perhaps this pressure is very weak because these instrumental benefits are not very important, at least in comparison to other considerations.\""}
(4) [Then genealogy adds nothing]: If the instrumental benefits are not very important, Pragmatic Genealogy is not adding much of anything to the humanist's toolkit.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"then it does not seem like Pragmatic Genealogy is adding much of anything to the humanist's toolkit\"", note: "The antecedent and the consequent are two sentences apart in the source: the condition is stated as `Perhaps this pressure is very weak because these instrumental benefits are not very important`, and this claim joins it to what the author says follows."}
-----
(5) [Genealogy is not crucial to humanistic philosophy]: Pragmatic Genealogy is not crucial to pursuing philosophy as a humanistic discipline, as Queloz claims.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"it is not crucial to pursuing philosophy as a humanistic discipline, as Queloz claims\""}

[Explanation does not alleviate the tension]: Shifting from justification to explanation does not do much to alleviate the tension between the humanistic sensibility and the engineering sensibility.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 382", source: "\"shifting from justification to explanation does not do much to alleviate the tension between the humanistic sensibility and the engineering sensibility\"", note: "Introduced by `The point is that`, which marks the section's summary rather than a further inference from the dilemma's conclusion."}
    <+ [Genealogy is not crucial to humanistic philosophy]

<Either the pressure is real or the genealogy adds nothing>
    -> <Explanation without justification reconciles the two>

## What the method cannot see

<Genealogy neglects constitutive relations>: Instrumental relations are not the only ones among our values, and the ones a pragmatic genealogy cannot see are the ones humanistic moral theory works with.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 383", note: "The section's last objection, and the cashing-out of a point left hanging on p. 373."}

<Genealogy neglects constitutive relations>

(1) [Focus directs attention away]: A focus on instrumental relations directs attention away from important symbolic and constitutive relations among our values.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 383", source: "\"such a focus directs attention away from important symbolic and constitutive relations among our values\""}
(2) [Norms may constitute mutual recognition]: Adhering to certain norms may constitute a valuable relation of mutual recognition. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 383", source: "\"adhering to certain norms constitutes a valuable relation of mutual recognition\""}
(3) [Genealogy cannot grip such relations]: Pragmatic Genealogy, by its nature, does not have a way of getting a grip on constitutive and symbolic relations and so tends to neglect them.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 383", source: "\"These are the kinds of relations that Pragmatic Genealogy, by its nature, does not have a way of getting a grip on and so, therefore, tends to neglect.\""}
(4) [Humanistic theory has interpretive elements]: Good humanistic moral theory typically has interpretive elements that Pragmatic Genealogy ignores.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 383", source: "\"good humanistic moral theory typically has interpretive elements that Pragmatic Genealogy ignores\""}
-----
(5) [The neglect should give the humanist pause]: The neglect of constitutive and symbolic relations might give the humanist pause.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 383", source: "\"This neglect might give the humanist pause.\""}

[Genealogy cannot grip such relations]
    <+ [Pragmatic Genealogy cannot illuminate constitutive relations]

<Widen the notion of function>: A pragmatic approach could work with a notion of function capacious enough to take in constitutive and expressive relations. #contested
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "n. 29", note: "The author raises the objection against himself in a footnote and answers it with a dilemma. Left as a sketch: the footnote concedes the possibility in one clause and spends the rest of its length denying that it helps."}

[A more capacious notion of function]: It is theoretically possible for a pragmatic approach to adopt a more capacious notion of 'function', one that might encompass things like constitutive and expressive relations. #contested
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "n. 29", source: "\"one that might encompass things like constitutive and expressive relations\""}

[Genealogy cannot grip such relations]
    <- <Widen the notion of function>

<The structure dilemma>: Build less structure into the pragmatic approach and it stops being distinctive; build more and it begs the questions it was meant to clarify.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "n. 29", note: "AN UNDERCUT. The footnote grants that the notion of function could be widened, and denies that widening it leaves anything worth having — so the objection's premise stands and its point does not."}

<The structure dilemma>

(1) [A more capacious notion of function] #contested
(2) [Capaciousness costs privilege]: If a pragmatic approach adopts a more capacious notion of function, Pragmatic Genealogy is no longer a privileged tool for answering the Pragmatic Question.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "n. 29", source: "\"if we do this, Pragmatic Genealogy is no longer a privileged tool for answering the Pragmatic Question\""}
(3) [Less structure, less distinctive]: The less structure we build into the pragmatic approach, the less distinctive it is from the Socratic approach and the less it can be used to clarify Socratic debates.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "n. 29", source: "\"the less structure we build into the pragmatic approach, the less distinctive it is from the Socratic approach and the less it can be used to clarify Socratic debates\""}
(4) [More structure, begs the questions]: If we build more structure into the pragmatic approach, it will beg and obscure many of the crucial philosophical questions.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "n. 29", source: "\"if we build more structure into the pragmatic approach, it will beg and obscure many of the crucial philosophical questions\""}
-----
(5) [Widening the notion does not help]: Adopting a more capacious notion of function does not rescue the pragmatic approach.
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", pinpoint: "n. 29", note: "The footnote sets out both horns and leaves the disjunction's upshot unstated."}

<The structure dilemma>
    _> <Widen the notion of function>

[The method has certain limits]
    <+ [Genealogy is not crucial to humanistic philosophy]
    <- [The third concern is not pursued] #conceded
